"As to the ShinyApp question, it really depends on the nature of your web server. For instance, if you web server is on linux, you cannot just copy the shinyapp generated from windows. You'll have to re-generate a shinyapp with RxODE on the native web server & load it per your web server configuration. You may have to consult your web master how to get this step."

The following app.R script runs fine locally on my Windows computer with Rtools installed, but won't run properly on shinyapps.io <http://shinyapps.io> with this error message:
"ERROR: Error sourcing /srv/connect/apps/myapp/app.R"

#Load packages
library(shiny)
library(RxODE)
#Model
ode <- "
  Cp = B/Vc;
  K10 = Cl/Vc;
  d/dt(A) = -Ka*A;
  d/dt(B) =  Ka*A -K10*B;
"
tmp <- tempfile()
m1 <- RxODE(model=ode, modName="m1", wd=tmp)
inits <- c(A=0,B=0,C=0)
tha = c(Ka = 2.39,Cl = 0.315,Vc = 0.516)
#UI
ui <- fixedPage(
    sidebarLayout(
      sidebarPanel(
        sliderInput("DOSE","Dose (mg):",min = 0, max = 10, value = 5, step = 1)),
      mainPanel(plotOutput("plot1"))
    )
)
#Server
server <-(function(input, output){
  Dose = reactive({input$DOSE})
  sim <- reactive({
    dose <- Dose()
    ev <- eventTable(amount.units="µg", time.units="hours")
    ev$add.sampling(seq(0,24,0.5))
    ev$add.dosing(dose=d,dosing.to <http://dosing.to>=1,nbr.doses=1,dosing.interval=24)
    sim <- m1$solve(tha,ev,inits)})
  output$plot1 <- renderPlot({
    x <- sim()
    plot(x[,"time"],x[,"Cp"],type="l",xlab="Time",ylab="Conc")})
})

Thanks!

